# People-first content

Applies when writing or editing a URL that strangers will read (site, docs,
blog, changelog that doubles as marketing).

- Write for a named audience that would use this page if they arrived
  directly. Do not write a page whose only job is to rank or to be cited.
- Bring a unique point of view: first-hand experience, original data, or
  analysis that is not a rewrite of the ten results already ranking.
- Answer the question completely. A reader should not need a second search
  to finish the job.
- Show who wrote it, how it was made (including material AI use), and why
  it exists. Trust first. Experience, expertise, and authority support it.
- YMYL topics (money, health, safety, civic) need identifiable experts and
  sources. Do not ship thin advice there.
- Title and H1 describe the page. No clickbait, no date-bump without a
  real change.
- Commodity listicles ("7 tips anyone could write") are a miss. Either add
  proof only this team has, or do not publish.
- After the draft, run `writing-citable-content` for extractable structure
  and `cleaning-ai-copy` if the prose still sounds like a model.
